<div class="article-body">

<p>You checked the water. You added the nutrients. You set up the light on a timer. Everything looked good for a week. Then the leaves turned yellow. Then brown. Then your plant died.</p>

<p>I know this feeling because I killed my first 3 hydroponic setups. I followed YouTube tutorials, bought the recommended nutrients, and still ended up with a slimy mess that smelled like a swamp. I almost quit.</p>

<p>Here is the thing: hydroponic plants do not die for mysterious reasons. They die for 5 specific, fixable reasons. Once you know which one you are dealing with, the fix takes under 10 minutes. I will show you exactly how to diagnose each problem and what to do about it.</p>

<h2 id="yellow">1. Yellow Leaves (The #1 Sign of Trouble)</h2>

<p>Yellow leaves are the most common problem in hydroponics. I have seen this on Reddit a hundred times: "My lettuce was fine for 2 weeks, now the lower leaves are turning yellow. Help."</p>

<p><strong>What it looks like:</strong> Lower leaves turn pale yellow first. Upper leaves stay green. The yellowing moves up the plant over days.</p>

<p><strong>The most likely cause:</strong> Nitrogen deficiency. Not enough nutrients in the water, or the pH is wrong and your plant cannot absorb them.</p>

<p>Here is the key insight: your plant can have the best nutrient solution in the world, but if the pH is outside the sweet spot, it cannot drink. It is like putting premium gas in a car with a clogged fuel line. The gas is fine. The car still stalls.</p>

<p><strong>Your 10-minute fix:</strong></p>
<p>Step 1. Grab a pH test kit or meter. I use a $12 liquid test kit from Amazon, but a $25 digital meter is faster.</p>
<p>Step 2. Test your reservoir water. For almost all leafy greens and herbs, the sweet spot is <strong>5.5 to 6.5</strong>. If your reading is above 7.0 or below 5.0, that is your problem.</p>
<p>Step 3. Add pH up or pH down. Add 1ml at a time, stir, wait 5 minutes, test again. Repeat until you hit 5.5-6.5.</p>
<p>Step 4. If the pH was fine, add a half-strength dose of your nutrient solution. Sometimes you just need to feed them.</p>

<p>You should see results in 48 hours. The yellowing will stop spreading. New growth will come in green. The old yellow leaves will not turn green again, but the plant will recover.</p>

<p><strong>But here is the part I did not expect:</strong> sometimes yellow leaves are not a deficiency. They happen when leaves are old and the plant is focusing energy on new growth. If only the bottom-most leaves are yellow and everything above looks healthy, you might not have a problem at all. That is just your plant getting older. Let them fall off naturally.</p>

<p>Pay attention to this number: if more than 30% of the plant is yellow, it is a deficiency. If only the bottom 2 leaves are yellow, it is normal aging.</p>

<h2 id="rot">2. Brown Slimy Roots (Root Rot)</h2>

<p>You lift your net pot and smell it. A rotting swamp smell. The roots that were white and fuzzy are now brown, mushy, and slimy. This is root rot. It is the second most common killer of hydroponic plants, and it spreads fast.</p>

<p><strong>What it looks like:</strong> Roots turn from white to light brown to dark brown. They feel slippery. The water in your reservoir may smell bad. The plant will wilt even though you just watered it.</p>

<p><strong>The cause:</strong> Bacteria and fungi growing in your reservoir. This happens when water temperature gets above 72F (22C), when light hits the water, or when dead plant matter sits in the reservoir and rots.</p>

<p>Imagine leaving a glass of milk on your counter for a week. That is what warm, light-exposed water becomes for your roots.</p>

<p><strong>Your 10-minute fix:</strong></p>
<p>Step 1. Remove the affected plant and rinse the roots under cool tap water. Trim off the mushy parts with clean scissors. If more than 50% of the roots are brown, the plant may not recover. Start a new cutting.</p>
<p>Step 2. Empty and clean the reservoir. Use a 3% hydrogen peroxide solution (1 part peroxide to 3 parts water) to sterilize it. Rinse well.</p>
<p>Step 3. Add fresh water and nutrients. Add a root health supplement. I use Southern Ag Garden Friendly Fungicide, which is just beneficial bacteria that eat the bad bacteria. Cost me $15 on Amazon and has saved 6 plants since.</p>
<p>Step 4. Fix the root cause: your water is too warm. Hydroponic reservoirs should stay between 65F and 72F. If your reservoir sits above that, move it away from heat sources or add a frozen water bottle in summer.</p>

<p>Here is the honest truth: root rot is the hardest problem to fix once it takes hold. Prevention is everything. If you lose a plant to root rot, do not blame yourself. It happens to everyone. I lost a full tray of kale to it in my third month. The fix that actually worked was switching to an opaque reservoir and adding beneficial bacteria from the start.</p>

<h2 id="stunted">3. Plants Stopped Growing After Week 2</h2>

<p>Your seedlings sprouted. They looked great for 10 days. Then they just stopped. No new leaves. No growth. Just sitting there, alive but not moving.</p>

<p>This one frustrated me the most because nothing looked wrong. No yellow leaves. No rot. Just a plant that refused to grow.</p>

<p><strong>What it looks like:</strong> Plant is alive and green, but has not grown in 5+ days. Leaves may look dark green or purplish. New growth is tiny or missing.</p>

<p><strong>Most likely cause:</strong> The roots hit the water line but the oxygen level is too low. Or the plant has outgrown its container and needs more room.</p>

<p>This is where it gets interesting: hydroponic plants need oxygen at their roots more than they need nutrients. If the water is stagnant or the roots are drowning, the plant stalls. It is conserving energy because it cannot breathe.</p>

<p><strong>Your 10-minute fix:</strong></p>
<p>Step 1. Check your water level. The roots should be submerged but the stem crown should be above water. If the water is too high, the stem will rot. If too low, roots dry out. The ideal: roots are in water, the top of the net pot is dry.</p>
<p>Step 2. Add an air stone. A simple aquarium air stone + pump costs $10 on Amazon. Put it in your reservoir and run it 24/7. I did this and saw new growth in 3 days.</p>
<p>Step 3. If you already have an air stone, check that it is actually bubbling. I once had a $6 pump fail silently for 2 weeks before I noticed.</p>
<p>Step 4. Check your nutrient strength. Use a TDS meter ($15 on Amazon). For seedlings, 200-400 PPM. For mature leafy greens, 600-900 PPM. If you are below 200, feed them. If above 1200, you are burning them.</p>

<h2 id="algae">4. Green Slime in Your Reservoir</h2>

<p>You open your reservoir and find green gloop on the walls. Maybe floating in the water. This is algae. It is not directly killing your plants, but it competes for nutrients and oxygen. And it is a sign that light is getting where it should not.</p>

<p><strong>What it looks like:</strong> Bright green film on reservoir walls, net pots, or clay pebbles. Water may turn greenish. Slime feels slippery.</p>

<p><strong>The cause:</strong> Light hitting your nutrient solution. Algae need light and water. If you are using a clear reservoir, transparent tubing, or uncovered net pots, algae will grow.</p>

<p><strong>Your 10-minute fix:</strong></p>
<p>Step 1. Block the light. Wrap your reservoir in black plastic, duct tape, or aluminum foil. Even a clear plastic container will grow algae in 3 days.</p>
<p>Step 2. Cover the top of your net pots. Use hydroton or clay pebbles to block light from reaching the water through the pot opening. Or cut a piece of black plastic to fit around the stem.</p>
<p>Step 3. Add 1 teaspoon of 3% hydrogen peroxide per gallon of water. It kills algae on contact and breaks down into water and oxygen within hours. Safe for plants.</p>

<p>Here is why that matters: algae is not dangerous by itself. But it signals that your system has conditions that could lead to root rot. Fix the light leak now. Do not wait until you smell the swamp smell.</p>

<h2 id="wilt">5. Leaves Curling, Drooping, or Burning</h2>

<p>Your leaves look weird. Curling down at the edges. Drooping even though the roots are wet. Brown crispy tips. This one scared me because I thought my plants were catching a disease.</p>

<p><strong>What it looks like:</strong> Leaf edges curl downward or upward. Tips turn brown and crispy. Leaves may droop during the day and recover at night. White or yellow burn spots on leaves near the light.</p>

<p><strong>The causes and fixes:</strong></p>

<p><strong>Cause A: Light burn.</strong> Your light is too close or too powerful. This is the most common cause of leaf curling in hydroponics. New growers think more light = more growth.</p>
<p>Fix: Move your light up. For LED panels, 12 to 18 inches above the plants. For T5 tubes, 6 to 12 inches. If leaves show white or pale bleached spots, the light is literally cooking them. Move it up and watch for recovery in 2 days.</p>

<p><strong>Cause B: Nutrient burn.</strong> You added too much fertilizer. The leaf tips turn brown and crispy. This happens when TDS goes above 1000 PPM for leafy greens.</p>
<p>Fix: Drain and replace with plain pH-balanced water. Run that for 24 hours. Then add half the recommended nutrient dose and work up from there. I did this with a batch of basil that I overdosed. They looked sad for 3 days, then bounced back fully.</p>

<p><strong>Cause C: Temperature stress.</strong> Your grow space is too hot or too cold above 85F or below 60F.</p>
<p>Fix: Check air temperature at plant height. If it is above 80F, add a fan. Below 65F, move the setup to a warmer room. Most leafy greens grow best between 68F and 75F.</p>

<p><strong>Cause D: Lack of air circulation.</strong> Drooping leaves with no other symptoms.</p>
<p>Fix: Add a small USB fan pointing across the plants, not directly at them. Moving air strengthens stems, prevents mold, and helps plants breathe. A $7 clip fan changed everything for me.</p>

<h2 id="prevent">6. How to Stop Problems Before They Start</h2>

<p>I spent 4 months killing plants before I learned this: 90% of hydroponic problems are preventable with 3 things. Spend the $30 now. Save the frustration later.</p>

<p><strong>The 3 things every beginner needs:</strong></p>

<p>1. A $12 pH test kit. Check pH every time you refill. This alone prevents 60% of problems.</p>
<p>2. A $15 TDS meter. Tells you exact nutrient strength. Feed too little, plants starve. Feed too much, plants burn.</p>
<p>3. An opaque reservoir. Clear containers grow algae. Black or blue containers do not. If you already have a clear container, wrap it in duct tape.</p>

<p><strong>Weekly check routine (takes 5 minutes):</strong></p>
<p>Monday morning: Check pH. Check water level. Check roots (lift a net pot, look for white).</p>
<p>Thursday morning: Top off with pH-balanced water. Check TDS. Look at leaves.</p>
<p>That is it. 5 minutes, twice a week. If you do this, you will catch 90% of problems before they kill your plant.</p>

<p>Here is the part that matters: every single plant I killed taught me something. The yellow lettuce taught me about pH. The root rot taught me about water temperature. The stunted basil taught me about oxygen.</p>

<p>You will kill some plants. That is how this works. But after reading this guide, you will know what went wrong and what to fix. And next time, you will catch it before the leaves turn yellow.</p>

